WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.079
Navigation interference threatens more than the map display.

00:00:04.079 --> 00:00:15.885
It can affect position hold, automated routes, return-to-home, geofencing, camera targeting, mapping overlap, flight logs, timestamps, and the crew's shared understanding of where the aircraft is.

00:00:15.885 --> 00:00:19.534
Automated flight can continue using incorrect assumptions.

00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:23.612
A waypoint route may diverge, pause, or terminate.

00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:31.555
The pilot may spend critical time interpreting warnings while battery, distance, wind, and obstacles continue to change.

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:33.916
Return-to-home deserves special caution.

00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:43.146
It depends on a correct home point and trustworthy current position, along with navigation, return altitude, wind, and obstacle assumptions.

00:00:43.146 --> 00:00:49.801
If those inputs are doubtful, automatic return may create risk rather than solve it.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:56.026
Geotagging and map products can become unreliable even when visible imagery remains useful.

00:00:56.026 --> 00:01:01.821
Analysts should not treat location metadata as authoritative after a navigation anomaly.

00:01:01.821 --> 00:01:05.471
Flag affected data for review and corroboration.

00:01:05.471 --> 00:01:09.120
Downstream systems may ingest those records automatically.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:20.497
Notify mapping, inspection, evidence, or analytics owners when location or time confidence is reduced so questionable data does not silently become an authoritative business record.

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:23.717
Crew confusion can amplify the hazard.

00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:29.083
One person may trust the screen while another trusts visual cues.

00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:36.596
Predefined communication and authority help the pilot prioritize aircraft control instead of debating attribution in flight.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:41.533
Safety priorities are people, other aircraft, property, and controlled recovery.

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:44.753
Mission data and equipment come afterward.

00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:54.842
Reduce dependence on automation, preserve orientation and battery margin, select a safe landing, and document the anomaly once immediate risk is controlled.
